@@ -0,0 +1,140 @@
+# Shared expenses service
+
+A small HTTP service that records shared expenses for groups and works out who owes whom.
+It is a single file, `server.js`, using Node.js built-ins only (no npm dependencies).
+
+## Running it
+
+```sh
+PORT=3000 DATA_FILE=./expenses.json node server.js
+```
+
+- `PORT` is the TCP port. `PORT=0` lets the operating system pick one. Either way the server
+  prints `Listening on port <n>` on standard output once it is ready.
+- `DATA_FILE` is the JSON file that holds the groups and expenses. They are read back from it at
+  startup, so they survive a restart.
+- Both are required. If either is missing, or `PORT` is not a whole number from 0 to 65535, the
+  process stops with an error before listening.
+- Tested on Node v26.12.1. Balance and settlement answers use `JSON.rawJSON`, so the Node version
+  must provide it.
+- The server listens on all network interfaces, not only on loopback, and has no authentication.
+
+Run the tests with `node --test` from this folder.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+Every answer is JSON. Error answers are `{"error": "<message>"}`.
+
+### POST /groups
+
+Body: `{"id": "<group id>", "members": ["<name>", ...]}`
+
+| Status | When |
+| --- | --- |
+| 201 `{"id": "<group id>"}` | The group was created. |
+| 409 | A group with this id already exists. This is checked before the members, so a duplicate id answers 409 even when the members are invalid. |
+| 400 | The body is not a JSON object; the id is not a string with at least one non-whitespace character; or `members` is not a list of at least two distinct names, each a string with at least one non-whitespace character. |
+| 500 | The data file could not be written; the group was not created. |
+
+### POST /groups/&lt;id&gt;/expenses
+
+Equal split, shared among all members:
+
+```json
+{"payer": "Ada", "cents": 10, "split": "equal"}
+```
+
+Each member owes `cents` divided by the number of members, rounded down. The cents left over go one
+each to the members listed first.
+
+Exact split:
+
+```json
+{"payer": "Ada", "cents": 1000, "split": "exact", "shares": {"Bea": 700, "Cam": 300}}
+```
+
+Each member listed in `shares` owes that many cents (0 is allowed); members not listed owe 0. The
+shares must add up to `cents`. The payer may be listed.
+
+| Status | When |
+| --- | --- |
+| 201 `{"expense": <n>}` | The expense was recorded. `n` is 1 for the group's first expense, then 2, and so on. |
+| 400 | The body is not JSON; the payer or a name in `shares` is not a member; `cents` is not a positive whole number; a share is not a whole number of 0 or more; `split` is not `equal` or `exact`; an exact split has no `shares` object; or the shares do not add up to `cents`. A rejected expense does not use up a number. |
+| 404 | The group does not exist (checked before the body). |
+| 500 | The data file could not be written; nothing was recorded. |
+
+Expenses posted to the same group at the same moment are all recorded, each with its own number.
+
+### GET /groups/&lt;id&gt;/balances
+
+200 with each member's net cents, what they paid minus what they owe, in member order. The values
+add up to 0. After the equal-split example above in a group of Ada, Bea and Cam:
+`{"Ada": 6, "Bea": -3, "Cam": -3}`. 404 if the group does not exist.
+
+### GET /groups/&lt;id&gt;/settlements
+
+200 with a list of payments that bring every balance to 0. For the balances above:
+`[{"from": "Bea", "to": "Ada", "cents": 3}, {"from": "Cam", "to": "Ada", "cents": 3}]`. Repeatedly, the member who owes most pays the member
+who is owed most the smaller of the two amounts; a tie goes to the member listed first. The list is
+`[]` when every balance is 0. 404 if the group does not exist.
+
+Any other method or path answers 404.
+
+## Behaviour worth knowing
+
+### Names and group ids
+
+- Ids and names are compared exactly as sent, including case and surrounding spaces: `"Ada"`,
+  `"ada"` and `" Ada"` are three different members.
+- A name repeated in `members` counts once, at its first position. That position decides who gets
+  leftover cents and who wins ties.
+- In a URL the id is one path segment, percent-decoded once. An id such as `room/1` is addressed as
+  `/groups/room%2F1/balances`. The query string is ignored. A trailing slash or an extra path
+  segment answers 404.
+- Ids are not otherwise restricted, and some ids that creation accepts with 201 cannot be used
+  from ordinary HTTP clients:
+  - `.` and `..`: URL libraries (Node's `fetch`, for example) collapse these segments, also when
+    written as `%2E`, so the request reaches another path and answers 404. Only a client that sends
+    the path unchanged (such as `curl --path-as-is`) reaches the group.
+  - An id containing an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate, such as `"\ud800"`, has no valid URL form; its
+    routes answer 404.
+  - A very long id makes the request line exceed Node's 16 KB header limit, and that group's
+    routes answer 431. A 16,000-character id worked; 16,340 characters failed on balances, and
+    16,300 failed on expenses.
+
+  Use short ids made of URL-safe characters.
+
+### Request bodies and amounts
+
+- Bodies are read as JSON whatever the `Content-Type` header says. Unknown properties are ignored,
+  and an equal split ignores any `shares` sent with it.
+- Amounts in requests are whole cents up to 9,007,199,254,740,991 (JavaScript's safe-integer
+  limit). A larger amount answers 400.
+- A number counts as whole only if it is exactly whole as written. `2`, `2.0`, `2e0` and `1.5e1`
+  are accepted. `1.5`, `0.99999999999999999`, `2.0000000000000001` and `-1e-400` answer 400, even
+  though JavaScript would round some of them to whole numbers. `-0` is rejected as `cents` and
+  counts as 0 as a share.
+- Balances and settlement amounts are sums, so they can exceed the safe-integer limit. They are
+  written as exact JSON integers, for example `18014398509481982`. A client handling totals that
+  large needs a JSON parser that keeps integer precision.
+
+### The data file
+
+- Run one process per `DATA_FILE`. The service does not lock the file and does not notice a second
+  process using it. Each save replaces the whole file, so with two processes on one file the last
+  save wins and expenses the other process acknowledged are lost. In a test, two expenses sent to
+  two processes were each answered 201 with number 1; after a restart only one of them was left.
+  Stop the old process before starting a new one on the same file.
+- A missing file is created by the first successful write. If its directory does not exist, the
+  server still starts and prints its port, and the first write answers 500.
+- At startup, an existing file that is not valid JSON, or is JSON without a `groups` list (so not
+  a ledger this service wrote), stops the process with an error before it listens. The file is
+  left unchanged.
+- Each save writes a new file next to the ledger and renames it over the ledger, so the ledger ends
+  up readable and writable by its owner only (mode 0600). An existing file with mode 0640 becomes
+  0600 after the first save.
+- If `DATA_FILE` is a symlink to an existing file, saves go to that file and the link stays in
+  place. A symlink whose target does not exist yet is replaced by a regular file on the first save.
+- A failed save answers 500, records nothing and leaves balances and numbering as they were. The
+  reason is logged on standard error. After fixing the path or permissions, the same request can be
+  sent again.
